          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \dashv G \dashv H
            is called an
            %em ambidextrous adjunction
            (or sometimes
            %em ambiadjunction
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em ambijunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \simeq H
          %p
            In fact, often
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            %em identified
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %em strongly adjoint pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F \dashv G \dashv F)
            originally considered by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Morita65"} Morita 1965
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+functor"} Frobenius functor
            (ie. a functor which has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            that is also a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %em property
            of a pair of functors, rather than
            %em structure
            on a triple of functors.
          %p
            Sometimes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is said to be
            %em biadjoint
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            (not to be confused with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biadjoint"} biadjoint
            in the sense of
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biadjunction"} biadjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            which has a left and right adjoint which are equivalent is said to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+functor"} Frobenius functor
          %p
            In the special case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
            with an ambidextrous adjoint one also speaks of an
            %em essential localization
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bireflective+subcategory"} bireflective subcategory
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#frobenius_algebra_structure Frobenius algebra structure
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            induced by an ambidextrous adjunction is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monoid"} Frobenius monoid
            object in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctors"} endofunctors
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Lauda05"} Lauda 05, theorem 17
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monad"} Frobenius monad
          %h3#FiberwiseCharacterization Fiberwise characterization of ambidextrous Kan extension
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D} \in Cat_\infty
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            with small
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
            a morphism of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f^\ast \;\colon\; [Y,\mathcal{D}] \longrightarrow [X,\mathcal{D}]
          %p
            for the induced pullback of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-functor (∞,1)-categories
            (which one may think of as the categories of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+systems"} local systems
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            (if it exists) of this are left and right
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-Kan+extension"} (∞,1)-Kan extension
          #AmbidextrousKanExtension.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Say that a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is
              = succeed "-ambidextrous" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_! \dashv f^\ast)
              is an ambidextrous adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_! \simeq f_\ast)
              and in addtion all pullbacks of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              satisfy some property (…).
            %p
              Say that an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in Grpd_\infty
              is
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
                \-ambidextrous
              if its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} terminal
              map is.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsLurie14"}> Hopkins &amp; Lurie 2014, def. 4.1.11
            )
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
              between
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
              = succeed "-ambidextrous," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AmbidextrousKanExtension"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_y
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsLurie14"}> Hopkins &amp; Lurie 2014, prop. 4.3.5
            )
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+functor"} self-adjoint functor
              forms an ambidextrous adjunction.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \dashv G
              is an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi G
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \dashv G \dashv F
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \dashv F \dashv G
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (coincident limits and colimits)
              %br/
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              any category and consider the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi const
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} const \mathcal{D} \longrightarrow [\mathcal{C}^{op}, \mathcal{D}]
              that sends objects to constant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
              with this value. Then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              of this functor is, if it exists, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              construction, and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              is, if it exists, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              construction. (See also at
              = succeed ".)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              over
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
              of shape
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              coincide with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              over these diagrams. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilimits"} bilimits
              (but see the discussion of the terminology there).
            %p
              In
              %a{:href => "#HopkinsLuire14"} Hopkins &amp; Lurie 2014, section 4.3
              such
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is called
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
                \-ambidextrous
              (or rather, they consider
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
              and hence call it a
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒟
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
                  \-ambidextrous space
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%28n%29-local+stable+homotopy+theory"} K(n)-local stable homotopy theory
          %p
          #FrobeniusReciprocity.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+reciprocity"}> Frobenius reciprocity
                )
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ι
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \xhookrightarrow{\iota} G
              a
              = succeed "-inclusion" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+of+a+subgroup"} index
              the left and right
              = succeed "-functors" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induced+representation"} induced representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mi Rep
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                    %mi Rep
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^\ast \colon G Rep \to H Rep
              forming
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restricted+representations"} restricted representations
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ι
                        %mo !
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi ι
                        %mo *
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi H
                      %mi Rep
                      %mo →
                      %mi G
                      %mi Rep
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota_! \simeq \iota_\ast \,\colon\, H Rep \to G Rep
              %a{:href => "induced+representation#Ambidexterity"} there
              for more).
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/yoga+of+six+functors"}> yoga of six functors
                )
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wirthm%C3%BCller+context"} Wirthmüller context
              in the presence of an un-twisted
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wirthm%C3%BCller+isomorphism"} Wirthmüller isomorphism
              is an ambidextrous adjunction.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm+map"} norm map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wirthm%C3%BCller+context"} Wirthmüller context
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+cohesion"} infinitesimal cohesion
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+functor"} Frobenius functor
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Ambidextrous adjunctions were maybe first considered under the name
            %em strongly adjoint pairs
            (of functors), in:
          %ul
            %li#Morita65
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kiiti+Morita"}> Kiiti Morita
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Adjoint pairs of functors and Frobenius extensions
              %strong 9
              202/208 (1965) 40-71 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/43698658"}> jstor:43698658
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The terminology “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+functors"}> Frobenius functors
            ” for “strongly adjoint pairs” is due to
          %ul
            %li#CaenepeelMilitaruZhu97
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefaan+Caenepeel"}> Stefaan Caenepeel
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gigel+Militaru"} Gigel Militaru
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shenglin+Zhu"} Shenglin Zhu
